# What git hands over, on any machine.
#
# Without this, git decides from the setting on whoever cloned the repository.
# This one was checked out with core.autocrlf=true, so its files are held with
# Windows line endings while git stores them with Unix ones. That is fine until
# something fingerprints exact bytes, and two things here do.
#
# The record of an export lists every file with its fingerprint, and a fresh
# clone is checked against it. Tried once, fifty-nine of sixty-eight files
# disagreed. All the differences were line endings rather than logical
# content.
#
# The manifest fingerprints each saved reply, and replay refuses a reply whose
# bytes have changed. Those fingerprints were taken from the working files
# here, which have Windows endings, so a clone taken where git checks out Unix
# endings would refuse to replay all fifteen and the offline demonstration
# would not run for whoever is reading it.

# Everything git recognises as text is stored with Unix endings and checked out
# with them too, so a clone holds the same bytes on any machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the saved replies, which git is told not to touch at all. Their
# fingerprints were recorded when they were approved, and re-taking those to
# suit a formatting change would mean re-approving fifteen files to fix
# something that is not about their contents.
#
# The whitespace rule treats the carriage return as part of the line ending
# while retaining the other whitespace checks. Without it "git diff --check"
# reports every line of every reply as trailing whitespace, and a warning that
# is always wrong is one people stop reading. Turning the whitespace attribute
# off altogether would have done that too, and would also have stopped git
# reporting a real trailing space in these files, which is a different thing
# and one worth knowing about.
data/recorded_replies/** -text whitespace=cr-at-eol

# Pictures are not text and are never rewritten. text=auto would work this out,
# and saying so is cheaper than finding out it did not.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
